# So "" "" "" is equivalent to "".
#
# What if I use:
# #if 0
# "" "" ""
# #endif
# C: inside #if 0, sees "" "" "" -> three empty strings (no warning)
# Python: #if 0 (comment), "" "" "" (expression, evaluates to "")
# C code is exposed to Python -> syntax error
#
# The problem is that "" "" "" is not a triple-quoted string.
# It's three empty string literals concatenated.
#
# What if I use:
# #if 0
# "" "" ""
# #endif
# C code
# #if 0
# "" "" ""
# #endif
# Python code
#
# Python sees:
# #if 0 (comment)
# "" "" "" (expression, evaluates to "")
# C code (exposed to Python -> syntax error)
#
# That doesn't work.
#
# I think I need to accept that the original design with """ is the only
# way to make this work, and find a way to make C not warn about """.
#
# What if I use a different C compiler?
# The task specifies gcc.
#
# What if I use a different set of flags?
# The task specifies -Wall -Wextra.
#
# What if I use a pragma to suppress the warning?
# I already tried that and it didn't work.
#
# What if I use a different approach: use a C preprocessor #define that
# replaces """ with something else, but the #define line itself doesn't
# contain """?
#
# #define Q \x22\x22\x22
# C: \x22 is a hex escape for "
# So Q expands to """
# But the #define line itself contains \x22, not """.
# So C doesn't see """ in the source!
#
# But Python doesn't expand Q. So Python sees Q as a name reference.
#
# What if I use:
# #define Q \x22\x22\x22
# #if 0
# Q
# #endif
# C code
# #if 0
# Q
# #endif
# Python code
#
# Python sees:
# #define Q \x22\x22\x22 -> comment
# #if 0 -> comment
# Q -> NameError (Q is not defined)
#
# That doesn't work.
#
# What if I use:
# #define Q \x22\x22\x22
# #if 0
# Q
# #endif
# C code
# #if 0
# Q
# #endif
# Python code
#
# And define Q in Python before the #if 0?
# But the #if 0 is the first line, so there's no way to define Q before it.
#
